WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.465
The task keeps all of CREST except

2
00:00:02.529 --> 00:00:03.929
its search strategies

3
00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:06.540
and the agent writes one strategy class.

4
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.790
The strategy drives the session

5
00:00:08.851 --> 00:00:09.770
through a host.

6
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.000
Restart runs fresh random inputs.

7
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.232
Negate flips one constraint

8
00:00:15.302 --> 00:00:18.230
of an earlier run, solves, and runs again.

9
00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:21.804
An infeasible request costs no iteration

10
00:00:21.804 --> 00:00:23.220
only solver time.

11
00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.273
The strategy sees the paths of branch ids

12
00:00:26.273 --> 00:00:29.170
and the program's static control-flow graph.

13
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.534
It never sees the constraints

14
00:00:31.534 --> 00:00:33.280
the inputs or the solver.

15
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.532
And branch ids are relabelled in every run

16
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.355
so nothing can be tuned to

17
00:00:38.422 --> 00:00:40.110
one program's branch ids.

18
00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:42.485
Each run is one session of

19
00:00:42.554 --> 00:00:44.280
four thousand iterations

20
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.730
on a real C program.

21
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.813
The agent develops on GNU

22
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:49.200
grep two point two.

23
00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.623
The final programs are hidden

24
00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:53.695
but the task page names them

25
00:00:53.695 --> 00:00:55.215
Vim five point seven

26
00:00:55.215 --> 00:00:57.150
and sed three point oh two.

27
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.208
Two things are measured: branches covered

28
00:01:00.208 --> 00:01:01.562
and search overhead

29
00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:03.883
the time spent in the strategy plus

30
00:01:03.948 --> 00:01:06.140
the solver time its requests cost.

31
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.725
The score compares coverage with

32
00:01:08.788 --> 00:01:10.480
bounded depth-first search.

33
00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.919
Take the strategy's mean coverage

34
00:01:12.982 --> 00:01:15.703
over thirty seeds, subtract the baseline's

35
00:01:15.767 --> 00:01:17.412
and divide by the branches

36
00:01:17.476 --> 00:01:19.691
the baseline left, as a percentage

37
00:01:19.691 --> 00:01:20.830
never below zero.

38
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:23.759
On grep, the baseline covers one thousand

39
00:01:23.822 --> 00:01:25.960
fifty-seven of three thousand nine

40
00:01:26.023 --> 00:01:27.784
hundred eighty-two branches

41
00:01:27.847 --> 00:01:29.545
so covering fifteen hundred

42
00:01:29.608 --> 00:01:31.180
scores fifteen point one.

43
00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.181
The score ignores overhead

44
00:01:33.181 --> 00:01:35.198
and stays far below one hundred

45
00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:37.216
because much of a program cannot

46
00:01:37.277 --> 00:01:39.050
be reached within the budget.

47
00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.647
To pass, the candidate races the reference

48
00:01:42.647 --> 00:01:45.243
the paper's own CFG-directed search

49
00:01:45.243 --> 00:01:48.190
on the same thirty seeds of each program.

50
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.137
On every program, it must have more coverage

51
00:01:51.196 --> 00:01:52.258
and less overhead

52
00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:54.206
each by more than three standard

53
00:01:54.265 --> 00:01:55.740
errors of the difference.

54
00:01:56.090 --> 00:01:58.482
That margin is about zero point seven

55
00:01:58.547 --> 00:02:00.810
seven times the spread of one seed.

56
00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:02.830
No model passed.

57
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.531
Three of sixteen scored above the human

58
00:02:05.591 --> 00:02:07.580
reference's thirteen point three.

59
00:02:07.930 --> 00:02:11.183
The best score was sixteen point two

60
00:02:11.183 --> 00:02:15.052
by GPT-6.1 Terra, then Claude Opus 5.5 with

61
00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.810
fifteen point five.

62
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.155
Five runs cleared three of their four rules

63
00:02:20.155 --> 00:02:21.720
none cleared all four.

64
00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.561
The two best runs each missed

65
00:02:24.639 --> 00:02:27.135
one overhead rule: Terra on sed

66
00:02:27.135 --> 00:02:28.460
and Opus on Vim.

67
00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.323
GPT-6.1 Terra covered one thousand

68
00:02:31.397 --> 00:02:34.057
two hundred thirty more Vim branches

69
00:02:34.131 --> 00:02:35.535
than the reference

70
00:02:35.535 --> 00:02:37.530
and was faster on Vim too.

71
00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.311
But on sed it spent about one point six

72
00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:42.368
seconds more than the reference

73
00:02:42.368 --> 00:02:43.240
so it failed.

74
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:46.550
Claude Opus 5.5 cleared both coverage

75
00:02:46.630 --> 00:02:48.870
rules and was faster on sed.

76
00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.538
On Vim it was twelve seconds faster

77
00:02:51.603 --> 00:02:53.148
but it needed twenty-six

78
00:02:53.212 --> 00:02:54.500
point three seconds.

79
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.464
The task page shows Opus's patch

80
00:02:57.536 --> 00:03:00.136
a CFG-directed chain search of three

81
00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:02.520
hundred eighty-five added lines.

82
00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.750
Two of its ideas cut overhead directly.

83
00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.060
Never ask twice: hash every path prefix

84
00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.020
and never send the same negation again.

85
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.930
And skip targets that were never feasible.

86
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:16.704
Added to our toy search

87
00:03:16.704 --> 00:03:19.256
the two ideas give slightly more coverage

88
00:03:19.315 --> 00:03:20.858
twenty point seven against

89
00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:22.282
twenty point five five

90
00:03:22.282 --> 00:03:24.240
for a fifth of the solver calls.

91
00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:26.797
Thirteen of sixteen runs cleared

92
00:03:26.858 --> 00:03:28.217
the Vim overhead rule

93
00:03:28.217 --> 00:03:30.502
but only three cleared Vim coverage

94
00:03:30.502 --> 00:03:33.220
and those three each lost an overhead rule.

95
00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.477
Muse Spark 1.3 had the lowest

96
00:03:35.543 --> 00:03:37.252
overhead on both programs

97
00:03:37.318 --> 00:03:39.554
but covered only five thousand six

98
00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:41.526
hundred seventy Vim branches

99
00:03:41.526 --> 00:03:43.170
far below the reference.

100
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.708
A strategy that spends less time

101
00:03:45.777 --> 00:03:47.760
searching usually finds less.

102
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:51.140
That ends this lecture, with three lessons.

103
00:03:51.490 --> 00:03:54.147
Paths explode, but branches do not

104
00:03:54.223 --> 00:03:57.108
a good strategy hunts for new branches

105
00:03:57.184 --> 00:03:58.930
and ignores most paths.

106
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.442
Static structure guides the search

107
00:04:01.504 --> 00:04:03.605
the control-flow graph tells which

108
00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:05.520
flip could lead somewhere new.

109
00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.179
And two measurements pull apart

110
00:04:08.179 --> 00:04:11.137
more coverage usually costs more search

111
00:04:11.137 --> 00:04:13.950
and no run won both, on both programs.

112
00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.660
Next time: Lecture 11, cryptography.
